619
00:40:06,650 --> 00:40:16,090
Scott: And I'm just saying if you have a small pot, right, you know, it's like breaking the spaghetti is an ideal because it will be too short and eating it will be more difficult.

620
00:40:16,230 --> 00:40:16,351
Scott: Yeah.

621
00:40:16,371 --> 00:40:20,626
Scott: But like if the pot is really small, you're making a small portion, you could break it.

622
00:40:20,686 --> 00:40:21,890
Scott: It's not going to taste different.

623
00:40:22,151 --> 00:40:23,116
Scott: It's not the end of the world.

624
00:40:23,157 --> 00:40:23,297
Scott: Right.

625
00:40:23,338 --> 00:40:25,269
Scott: But you do got to push it in.

626
00:40:25,651 --> 00:40:25,873
Scott: Right.

627
00:40:26,154 --> 00:40:28,469
Scott: Or get a big giant pot like restaurants have.

628
00:40:29,652 --> 00:40:33,346
Rym: What I like to the problem is I want to use the super big pot we've got.

629
00:40:33,366 --> 00:40:34,470
Rym: that's got the built in strainer.

630
00:40:34,590 --> 00:40:37,529
Rym: So you put the water, you put the strainer in, then you put the pasta in.

631
00:40:38,470 --> 00:40:43,349
Rym: But there is almost no universe where I am making that much spaghetti at any given time.

632
00:40:44,150 --> 00:40:45,350
Scott: Yeah, that's a lot of water to boil.

633
00:40:45,691 --> 00:40:48,550
Rym: Maybe if we do a dinner with all our friends, I'll cook a giant spaghetti.

634
00:40:49,132 --> 00:40:51,558
Scott: A lot of wasted energy boiling that water.

635
00:40:51,578 --> 00:40:55,690
Scott: A lot of extra salt to salt the water when it's too much water.

636
00:40:56,111 --> 00:41:01,950
Rym: I will admit, I used to be not great at cooking this kind of noodle because I used to always make it very simply.

637
00:41:02,050 --> 00:41:05,230
Rym: I'd make the pasta in the water and separately I'd make the sauce.

638
00:41:05,590 --> 00:41:10,430
Rym: And then I would usually either mix them on the plate or in some fashion like that.

639
00:41:10,490 --> 00:41:12,889
Rym: But they were basically two separate things that were combined at the end.

640
00:41:13,351 --> 00:41:27,070
Rym: And it wasn't until I was an adult that I learned the art of cooking some pasta and then putting the noodles right back into a dish and mixing everything together and doing further cooking and a more complex method to make a superior pasta.

641
00:41:27,574 --> 00:41:28,729
Scott: The basic method, yeah.

642
00:41:29,170 --> 00:41:34,430
Scott: But if you like a big thick spaghetti, I think the biggest baddest spaghetti is the pucatini.

643
00:41:34,570 --> 00:41:37,830
Scott: Which is the spaghetti that's so big that it has the tiny hole through the middle.

644
00:41:41,791 --> 00:41:46,449
Rym: Some of my favorite noodles though are like the noodles you get in Thai food in a pad siu.

645
00:41:47,675 --> 00:41:48,950
Scott: The big flat rice noodle.

646
00:41:49,390 --> 00:41:55,450
Rym: Big flat wide rice noodles soaked in as much sauce as you can soak them in is some of the best noodles there on earth.

647
00:41:56,350 --> 00:41:57,694
Scott: Yep, I do love a rice noodle.

648
00:41:58,717 --> 00:42:02,670
Scott: Something about a rice noodle is like you can just keep eating so much of it.

649
00:42:02,950 --> 00:42:04,200
Scott: Like for some reason it's not filling.

650
00:42:04,220 --> 00:42:05,510
Scott: Like a pasta will fill you up.

651
00:42:06,894 --> 00:42:11,747
Scott: But a rice noodle doesn't seem to fill up my stomach so I can eat, like if a dish has rice noodle.

652
00:42:11,788 --> 00:42:12,650
Scott: I can just keep going.

653
00:42:14,071 --> 00:42:17,221
Scott: Even though the noodle is so, you'd think it's like the noodle is so substantial.

654
00:42:17,241 --> 00:42:20,090
Scott: They're big and flat and there's so much rice there basically.

655
00:42:20,770 --> 00:42:23,170
Scott: Like how many grains of rice is one rice noodle?

656
00:42:23,810 --> 00:42:30,990
Rym: But at the same time if I'm even a little hungry, like I can eat a cup or two of rice without even thinking about it.

657
00:42:31,871 --> 00:42:36,330
Scott: I'm just saying it's like, you know, rice noodle is far out there.

658
00:42:36,731 --> 00:42:38,114
Rym: Yeah, it's a pretty good noodle.

659
00:42:38,154 --> 00:42:44,250
Rym: Similarly, like you go to Xi'an or get like a Chinese noodle, the spicy cumin lamb, wide noodle in Xi'an.

660
00:42:44,912 --> 00:42:47,550
Rym: Same kind of noodle in terms of how much I enjoy it.

661
00:42:47,771 --> 00:42:48,714
Scott: The big flat ones, yeah.

662
00:42:49,336 --> 00:42:50,259
Scott: Love the big flat ones.

663
00:42:50,300 --> 00:42:53,210
Scott: You seem to like the big noodle, not that you're not a small noodle.

664
00:42:53,670 --> 00:42:54,197
Rym: I'm not a small noodle guy.

665
00:42:54,217 --> 00:42:55,007
Rym: I've always liked big noodle.

666
00:42:55,990 --> 00:42:56,449
Scott: Big noodle.

667
00:42:56,671 --> 00:43:02,670
Rym: I think it partly, honestly, I'd never thought about it until now, but I think I actually can point to why.

668
00:43:03,772 --> 00:43:07,990
Rym: My entire life I've been super cardioactive, like tons of biking and running.

669
00:43:09,454 --> 00:43:12,030
Rym: I eat a ton of carbohydrates all the time.

670
00:43:12,510 --> 00:43:21,670
Rym: When I was a kid, like I would eat effectively like a whole pound of pasta for dinner and just as much garlic bread as the family made.

671
00:43:21,770 --> 00:43:24,068
Rym: Like I would eat all of it if they didn't stop me.

672
00:43:24,852 --> 00:43:26,478
Rym: And I think that's just translated my whole life.

673
00:43:26,538 --> 00:43:29,690
Rym: So when in doubt, bigger noodles means more carbohydrate.

674
00:43:30,130 --> 00:43:35,190
Rym: And I think I've just always gravitated toward bigger noodles and more noodles in my entire life, naturally.

675
00:43:38,132 --> 00:43:39,175
Scott: I don't know.

676
00:43:39,235 --> 00:43:41,682
Scott: Why would I like a bigger noodle more?

677
00:43:41,803 --> 00:43:43,467
Scott: I don't know if I do like it, but I just like a noodle.

678
00:43:43,788 --> 00:43:44,450
Rym: I don't really care.

679
00:43:44,811 --> 00:43:52,410
Rym: I find that if I think of my favorite noodles, I imagine big noodles far earlier than I imagined small noodles.

680
00:43:54,470 --> 00:44:06,228
Scott: If I think of noodles that I don't like, I think of the alphabet soup where it's like, you know, you just got like these little, I guess they still have it, right?

681
00:44:07,470 --> 00:44:15,550
Scott: It's like, you know, instead of putting actual noodles in a soup, you just put like these, you know, these little tiny bits of pasta shaped like alphabet letters in there.

682
00:44:16,191 --> 00:44:20,770
Scott: And it's sort of like, you know, it's like it's even hard to kind of eat them if they don't, because they're not big enough.

683
00:44:21,352 --> 00:44:24,020
Scott: It's like, it just sort of adds texture to a soup.

684
00:44:24,541 --> 00:44:25,785
Scott: That doesn't really add flavor.

685
00:44:25,805 --> 00:44:27,390
Scott: It doesn't really feel like you're eating anything.

686
00:44:27,690 --> 00:44:36,070
Rym: Well, like they, if you put the soup in your mouth, they will crush under your tongue to such a degree that they might as well not even be there.

687
00:44:36,910 --> 00:44:37,010
Scott: Yeah.

688
00:44:37,030 --> 00:44:39,820
Scott: And then they're all over, like there's little bits all over your mouth.

689
00:44:39,860 --> 00:44:42,770
Scott: Like it's like, you know, you're eating popcorn and all the bits are getting stuck.

690
00:44:43,110 --> 00:44:45,090
Rym: You're going to swallow a lot of them whole and it doesn't matter.

691
00:44:46,652 --> 00:44:46,912
Scott: Yeah.

692
00:44:46,993 --> 00:44:52,030
Scott: The other kind of noodle that I don't hate it, but I'm not a huge fan is the cold noodle, right?

693
00:44:52,150 --> 00:44:53,194
Rym: I love a cold noodle.

694
00:44:53,214 --> 00:44:55,724
Rym: Give me a cold noodle any day.

695
00:44:55,804 --> 00:44:57,330
Rym: Cold noodle with a bunch of good sauce.

696
00:44:58,350 --> 00:44:58,530
Scott: Yeah.

697
00:44:58,570 --> 00:45:07,550
Scott: You know, it's like the special, it's like the soba noodle, like in summer in Japan is like the, like the hot, it's not hot, but it's literally cold, right?

698
00:45:07,872 --> 00:45:09,342
Scott: But it's a very popular dish, right?

699
00:45:09,362 --> 00:45:10,470
Scott: Like cold soba noodles.

700
00:45:10,951 --> 00:45:17,730
Scott: But it's like, there's something about the noodle being cold that I'm just like, eh, I don't really want to eat the cold noodle.

701
00:45:17,910 --> 00:45:23,869
Rym: For me, the only one I'm not a huge fan of is, I'm not the biggest fan of glass noodles in particular, hot or cold.

702
00:45:23,929 --> 00:45:24,290
Rym: Partly.

703
00:45:24,570 --> 00:45:34,430
Scott: Well, the glass noodle, actually the slippery texture I kind of like is kind of fun, but they don't have that sort of rich, you know, wheaty or ricey kind of flavor to them.

704
00:45:34,610 --> 00:45:39,150
Scott: They're sort of, you know, they taste as, as like, as clear as they look.

705
00:45:39,290 --> 00:45:39,511
Rym: Yup.

706
00:45:39,591 --> 00:45:49,890
Rym: Like I could take them or leave them and I like dishes that use them, but if I was going to pick a noodle to cook, I would definitely do like a soba noodle over a glass noodle, just in a vacuum if you made me choose.

707
00:45:50,870 --> 00:45:50,971
Scott: Yeah.

708
00:45:51,031 --> 00:45:53,209
Scott: What is the glass noodle actually made of?

709
00:45:53,490 --> 00:45:53,892
Rym: Just starch.

710
00:45:54,615 --> 00:45:57,790
Rym: Like usually they basically just extracted starch in most cases.

711
00:45:58,790 --> 00:45:59,072
Rym: I see.

712
00:45:59,152 --> 00:46:02,290
Scott: So it's starch only, but without the other parts, right?

713
00:46:02,530 --> 00:46:02,671
Rym: Yeah.

714
00:46:03,756 --> 00:46:06,230
Rym: I think the other parts are important for a lot of the dishes I like.

715
00:46:06,731 --> 00:46:06,851
Scott: Yeah.

716
00:46:06,891 --> 00:46:09,896
Scott: Imagine if you made a bread that was like gluten only, but nothing else.

717
00:46:10,638 --> 00:46:11,479
Scott: It's like, why not?

718
00:46:11,539 --> 00:46:12,661
Scott: It's not a gluten free bread.

719
00:46:12,701 --> 00:46:14,144
Scott: It's like a hundred percent gluten bread.

720
00:46:14,184 --> 00:46:14,404
Scott: There's.

721
00:46:14,444 --> 00:46:17,610
Scott: no, none of the other parts of the wheat flour are in there.

722
00:46:19,011 --> 00:46:19,749
Scott: Just the gluten bread.

723
00:46:20,291 --> 00:46:21,052
Rym: I just found a video.

724
00:46:21,072 --> 00:46:29,630
Rym: a hundred percent gluten bread, but cold soba noodles are one of my favorite summer dishes.

725
00:46:30,391 --> 00:46:30,612
Scott: Yeah.

726
00:46:30,632 --> 00:46:31,596
Scott: It's a, it's popular.

727
00:46:31,636 --> 00:46:35,130
Scott: I'm just, you know, I like a soba that's not cold.

728
00:46:35,451 --> 00:46:44,107
Rym: Now it's not the slipperiness I don't like about the cellophane noodles though, because I also really like a soba, which those are big, like those big noodles are really slippery.

729
00:46:44,187 --> 00:46:45,490
Rym: Just the nature of those noodles.

730
00:46:50,051 --> 00:46:54,089
Rym: I'm salivating thinking about noodles cause it's dinner time and Emily's cooking something in the other room right now.

731
00:46:54,874 --> 00:46:55,350
Scott: Is it noodles?

732
00:46:56,830 --> 00:46:59,748
Rym: I'm salivating to the point that I gotta, I gotta do something about it.

733
00:47:00,931 --> 00:47:06,642
Scott: It is weird though that like you think about other foods and there's like, you know, a range in quality levels, right?

734
00:47:06,822 --> 00:47:10,469
Scott: You know, for like steak, you got like your wagyu all the way down to your McDonald's.

735
00:47:10,710 --> 00:47:10,850
Scott: Right.

736
00:47:11,531 --> 00:47:14,838
Scott: And it's like, there's a line that you have where it's like let below this line.

737
00:47:14,858 --> 00:47:15,539
Scott: I'm not eating that.

738
00:47:15,599 --> 00:47:16,261
Scott: That's right.

739
00:47:16,662 --> 00:47:20,470
Scott: But with noodle, it's like the line goes really low.

740
00:47:20,871 --> 00:47:23,977
Scott: It's like a box of pasta from the grocery store.

741
00:47:24,498 --> 00:47:25,280
Scott: Any brand.

742
00:47:25,320 --> 00:47:26,362
Scott: Literally doesn't matter.

743
00:47:26,763 --> 00:47:30,030
Rym: A brand I've never heard of that looks like it's from the seventies, probably fine.

744
00:47:31,050 --> 00:47:32,153
Scott: It's like, sure, I'll eat that.

745
00:47:32,213 --> 00:47:32,854
Scott: That seems good.

746
00:47:32,875 --> 00:47:33,035
Scott: Right?

747
00:47:33,255 --> 00:47:39,790
Scott: It's like Kraft, Kraft Mac and cheese is like, well, the cheese is questionable, but the Mac, it's like those noodles, their macaronis are fine.

748
00:47:39,970 --> 00:47:50,310
Rym: And all the way up to the top, the best fresh pasta is better than a dried pasta or like a cheaper pasta, but it's not that much better.

749
00:47:52,131 --> 00:47:52,892
Scott: It's, it's better.

750
00:47:52,932 --> 00:47:54,455
Scott: I would definitely choose it every time.

751
00:47:54,515 --> 00:47:54,756
Scott: Yeah.

752
00:47:54,896 --> 00:48:00,387
Scott: But it's like a dried box of pasta is still way good because it's still a delicious bread.

753
00:48:00,707 --> 00:48:00,988
Scott: Yep.

754
00:48:01,469 --> 00:48:02,010
Scott: Wet, wet bread.

755
00:48:05,351 --> 00:48:15,450
Rym: Of course, I guess this is this is an opportunity to talk a little bit about the instant noodle, especially the ramen, which, you know, so the instant ramen is like even a level below like the box of pasta.

756
00:48:15,530 --> 00:48:16,532
Rym: And it's still hella good.

757
00:48:16,673 --> 00:48:24,070
Rym: And despite being the kind of person who like won't eat fast food, like even if I'm really hungry, I will generally just not eat rather than eat at McDonald's.

758
00:48:25,010 --> 00:48:29,401
Rym: I'll eat bottom of the barrel ramen in a pinch and have no regrets about it.

759
00:48:29,421 --> 00:48:29,501
Rym: Yeah.

760
00:48:30,043 --> 00:48:31,406
Scott: You know, that's the thing is I buy different.

761
00:48:31,426 --> 00:48:33,030
Scott: I've had a lot of different instant ramens.

762
00:48:33,270 --> 00:48:40,785
Scott: And while the the broths vary in quality and flavor dramatically on the noodles also vary greatly.

763
00:48:41,126 --> 00:48:43,070
Scott: But the noodle is never bad.

764
00:48:43,190 --> 00:48:46,055
Rym: The cheapest maruchan, totally fine.

765
00:48:46,075 --> 00:48:46,797
Rym: We'd eat it right now.

766
00:48:46,857 --> 00:48:47,338
Rym: No regrets.

767
00:48:47,939 --> 00:48:52,507
Scott: The noodle part of any of them that I've ever had is great.

768
00:48:52,907 --> 00:48:53,809
Scott: It's just fine.

769
00:48:54,110 --> 00:48:54,350
Scott: Right.

770
00:48:54,931 --> 00:48:56,877
Scott: It's like there's nothing wrong with that part of it.

771
00:48:56,897 --> 00:48:59,284
Scott: There are some where I'm like the broth is terrible.

772
00:48:59,626 --> 00:49:01,030
Scott: The little flavor thing is terrible.

773
00:49:01,511 --> 00:49:05,981
Scott: But the noodle part, even in cup noodle, it's like great.

774
00:49:06,042 --> 00:49:09,510
Rym: I know it's funny because, you know, we started camping a lot and we do ultra light hiking.

775
00:49:09,790 --> 00:49:24,590
Rym: So when we cook on these trips, we tend to cook things that involve mostly boiling water because having enough fuel to do a simmer or like making a fire to do a simmer is annoying compared to having the ability to boil a bunch of water in a hurry and then use that for cooking.

776
00:49:25,490 --> 00:49:31,221
Rym: A lot of hiking people get the rations and they get all these very specialized hiking dishes.

777
00:49:31,802 --> 00:49:35,850
Rym: And we have migrated away from that to just bringing ramen.

778
00:49:36,391 --> 00:49:37,493
Rym: And it's totally fine.

779
00:49:37,653 --> 00:49:40,618
Rym: And there is something about being out in the middle of the woods.

780
00:49:41,099 --> 00:49:41,680
Rym: You're hungry.

781
00:49:41,900 --> 00:49:47,670
Rym: You spend the night and you're not going to cook like a big camp dinner because you're not like at a giant campsite with a giant fire.

782
00:49:48,410 --> 00:49:53,997
Rym: And in about two minutes, having instant ramen just available to eat nice and hot.

783
00:49:54,477 --> 00:49:55,198
Rym: It's so good.

784
00:49:55,338 --> 00:50:05,550
Rym: And I don't know why so many hikers insist on eating the sometimes questionable or bland nonsense that you can buy as rations when instant ramen is right there.

785
00:50:05,670 --> 00:50:06,571
Rym: And it's like a dollar.

786
00:50:09,055 --> 00:50:12,099
Scott: So when it comes to not instant ramen.

787
00:50:12,380 --> 00:50:12,540
Scott: Right.

788
00:50:12,560 --> 00:50:14,323
Scott: There is actually quite a bit of variety.

789
00:50:14,383 --> 00:50:14,703
Rym: Oh yeah.

790
00:50:14,723 --> 00:50:16,165
Rym: There is a noodle.

791
00:50:16,325 --> 00:50:16,566
Scott: Right.

792
00:50:16,586 --> 00:50:19,330
Scott: You have different like, you know, wheat contents.

793
00:50:19,410 --> 00:50:19,550
Scott: Right.

794
00:50:19,650 --> 00:50:19,830
Scott: You got.

795
00:50:19,870 --> 00:50:26,464
Scott: sometimes you got like, you know, a buckwheat noodle ramen or you got different hydration levels of the of the noodles that happen.

796
00:50:26,564 --> 00:50:26,824
Scott: Yeah.

797
00:50:27,005 --> 00:50:27,285
Scott: Right.

798
00:50:27,646 --> 00:50:29,590
Scott: You've got different shapes.

799
00:50:29,650 --> 00:50:30,992
Scott: So sometimes you get the round one.

800
00:50:31,293 --> 00:50:34,678
Scott: Sometimes you get the slightly flattened one and sometimes you get the straight ones.

801
00:50:34,758 --> 00:50:36,441
Scott: and sometimes you get the wavy ones.

802
00:50:36,961 --> 00:50:37,142
Scott: Right.

803
00:50:37,602 --> 00:50:42,530
Scott: I prefer the wavy one just because eating it is more convenient.

804
00:50:42,730 --> 00:50:47,278
Scott: The straight ones sometimes, you know, it's not so it's like it is.

805
00:50:47,618 --> 00:50:48,760
Scott: it's easy enough to grab them.

806
00:50:48,820 --> 00:50:49,722
Scott: I grab them just fine.

807
00:50:50,042 --> 00:50:54,750
Scott: But the wavy ones, I just like the wavy noodles better for some reason.

808
00:50:55,071 --> 00:50:55,352
Rym: I like.

809
00:50:55,392 --> 00:50:59,162
Rym: I like my ramen noodles to be a little bit crisp or like a little bit like.

810
00:50:59,182 --> 00:51:00,686
Rym: I don't like him to be completely soggy.

811
00:51:00,726 --> 00:51:01,950
Rym: I like him to still have a little bit of.

812
00:51:02,190 --> 00:51:04,375
Scott: Well, I think that's true of just all noodles in general.

813
00:51:04,415 --> 00:51:04,575
Scott: Right.

814
00:51:04,615 --> 00:51:08,424
Scott: It's like it's wet bread, but you can't wet it to the point where it's right.

815
00:51:08,664 --> 00:51:09,546
Scott: It has to be.

816
00:51:10,127 --> 00:51:11,250
Scott: it's like you had a bread.

817
00:51:11,590 --> 00:51:18,481
Scott: You took out all the moisture and you're going to add back the exact right amount of moisture to where it's tasty.

818
00:51:19,042 --> 00:51:19,463
Scott: And it's like.

819
00:51:19,503 --> 00:51:23,670
Scott: I think the al dente rule applies to basically all noodle.

820
00:51:24,131 --> 00:51:36,290
Scott: I can't think of a noodle that I don't whether from any country of any food culture of any type where I don't want it to be that al dente level where, you know, it's like you think of an actual bread.

821
00:51:36,550 --> 00:51:39,215
Scott: It's like there's a crust and then there's the inside.

822
00:51:39,415 --> 00:51:39,675
Scott: Right.

823
00:51:40,016 --> 00:51:48,450
Scott: It's like so a noodle should be sort of the same texture contrast where there's the hydrated part and then in the middle, not as hydrated.

824
00:51:49,391 --> 00:51:56,504
Rym: The danger, though, I think a slightly overcooked noodle is generally better than a two undercooked noodle, because if they're well undercooked.

825
00:51:56,524 --> 00:51:56,704
Rym: Yes.

826
00:51:57,105 --> 00:52:00,190
Rym: If you bite into a noodle that's not cooked enough, that's no bueno.

827
00:52:00,590 --> 00:52:03,215
Rym: But if you bite into a noodle that's a little overcooked, it's fine.

828
00:52:03,235 --> 00:52:03,496
Scott: Yes.

829
00:52:03,716 --> 00:52:10,770
Scott: It's basically it's like if the noodle has not been cooked enough yet, it's basically it's like absolutely inedible.

830
00:52:11,131 --> 00:52:11,331
Scott: Right.

831
00:52:11,411 --> 00:52:12,973
Scott: It's still not wet enough bread.

832
00:52:13,474 --> 00:52:23,570
Scott: And then it reaches a point where it's like it is now edible and it would be ideal to basically stop cooking it as quickly as possible and eat it once it reaches that line.

833
00:52:23,770 --> 00:52:25,274
Scott: It's like it goes from zero to one hundred.

834
00:52:25,374 --> 00:52:25,534
Scott: Yeah.

835
00:52:25,735 --> 00:52:27,539
Scott: And then once it hits one hundred, perfect.

836
00:52:27,960 --> 00:52:30,547
Scott: It slowly degrades like that's.

837
00:52:30,587 --> 00:52:31,910
Scott: only 90 percent is good now.

838
00:52:32,311 --> 00:52:33,634
Scott: Only 80 percent is good now.

839
00:52:33,954 --> 00:52:34,175
Scott: Right.

840
00:52:34,275 --> 00:52:34,636
Scott: Still good.

841
00:52:34,656 --> 00:52:35,919
Rym: It doesn't get good, but it's worse.

842
00:52:36,680 --> 00:52:37,602
Scott: It doesn't get ruined.

843
00:52:37,923 --> 00:52:38,163
Scott: Right.

844
00:52:38,504 --> 00:52:41,170
Scott: It takes quite a while for it to go to inedible again.

845
00:52:41,330 --> 00:52:41,410
Rym: Yeah.

846
00:52:41,430 --> 00:52:46,682
Rym: You got to hit that threshold because if you pull it out of the water and you drain it and it's undercooked, now you're fucked.

847
00:52:48,005 --> 00:52:50,150
Scott: You could probably put it back in some other boiling water.

848
00:52:50,250 --> 00:52:53,636
Rym: Yeah, but you probably dumped all your boiling water out when you dumped it into the calendar.

849
00:52:53,676 --> 00:52:54,357
Rym: That's the problem.

850
00:52:54,858 --> 00:52:56,622
Scott: Well, here's some noodle technique for you.

851
00:52:56,742 --> 00:53:01,230
Scott: is that you could use, you know, some sort of straining spatula and scoop it out of there.

852
00:53:01,330 --> 00:53:01,591
Rym: Yeah.

853
00:53:01,631 --> 00:53:01,811
Rym: Right.

854
00:53:02,352 --> 00:53:05,739
Scott: Is that rather than, you know, it's like, you know, it's like the different techniques.

855
00:53:05,779 --> 00:53:05,919
Scott: Right.

856
00:53:05,939 --> 00:53:11,130
Scott: You can take the you can pick up the whole pot and dump it into a calendar and now your water's gone.

857
00:53:11,690 --> 00:53:11,971
Scott: Right.

858
00:53:12,292 --> 00:53:15,199
Scott: Obviously, you would scoop some of the water out by itself to save.

859
00:53:15,239 --> 00:53:18,367
Rym: We have something catching the water I'm going to need for the cooking or the calendar.

860
00:53:18,387 --> 00:53:18,488
Rym: Right.

861
00:53:19,590 --> 00:53:28,370
Scott: But if you're like in a restaurant or if you're going to cook noodles again or you're not sure, it's like scoop the noodles out with something and the water is still there.

862
00:53:28,410 --> 00:53:32,318
Scott: You can throw another noodle in there and you can cook more noodles at the same water.

863
00:53:32,338 --> 00:53:33,000
Scott: Right.

864
00:53:33,160 --> 00:53:33,481
Scott: You don't.

865
00:53:33,521 --> 00:53:36,928
Scott: you don't need to dump the water out, you know, with the noodles in it.

866
00:53:37,008 --> 00:53:37,970
Scott: There's a better way.

867
00:53:38,210 --> 00:53:38,350
Scott: Yeah.

868
00:53:41,853 --> 00:53:42,333
Scott: Any other new.

869
00:53:42,393 --> 00:53:43,434
Scott: Oh, I could throw out.

870
00:53:43,474 --> 00:53:47,197
Scott: Give a shout out to the the the lovely udon noodle noodle.

871
00:53:47,217 --> 00:53:47,937
Rym: Such a good noodle.

872
00:53:48,598 --> 00:53:51,320
Scott: I love for its thickness, thickness, slipperiness.

873
00:53:51,700 --> 00:54:03,780
Scott: It's usually good off its cousin, the American Chinese food lo mein noodle, which seems to be the only difference between them seems to be that the lo mein noodle is a little eggier, I guess.

874
00:54:04,981 --> 00:54:07,225
Scott: And also isn't usually in a soup.

875
00:54:08,106 --> 00:54:09,288
Scott: Udon's usually in a soup.

876
00:54:09,689 --> 00:54:09,909
Scott: Right.

877
00:54:09,970 --> 00:54:13,816
Scott: Whereas a lo mein noodle is just sauced.

878
00:54:13,856 --> 00:54:15,800
Scott: It has a bunch of other foods in there.

879
00:54:16,261 --> 00:54:21,932
Rym: So I was about to say something and I decided before I said it that it's out of bounds for this episode.

880
00:54:22,954 --> 00:54:25,078
Rym: I'm going to argue that a gnocchi is not a noodle.

881
00:54:25,118 --> 00:54:25,840
Rym: It is a dumpling.

882
00:54:27,561 --> 00:54:27,921
Scott: I don't know.

883
00:54:27,982 --> 00:54:29,224
Scott: It's just a semantic argument.

884
00:54:29,244 --> 00:54:29,986
Scott: I mean, it's a pasta.

885
00:54:30,767 --> 00:54:31,128
Rym: But is it?

886
00:54:31,388 --> 00:54:35,196
Scott: I mean, it is, you know, in the terms of the wet bread argument.

887
00:54:35,296 --> 00:54:35,517
Scott: Right.

888
00:54:35,577 --> 00:54:37,200
Scott: It's like you could have potato bread.

889
00:54:37,681 --> 00:54:43,340
Rym: So it's like a wet potato bread because I just went to a restaurant supply store in bulk and gnocchi is listed under dumplings.

890
00:54:44,541 --> 00:54:44,861
Scott: Okay.

891
00:54:45,162 --> 00:54:45,322
Scott: Sure.

892
00:54:45,362 --> 00:54:54,440
Scott: I mean, you know, if I if I just take pastas and I, you know, of wheat and I make them into like a gnocchi shape, is that a dumpling?

893
00:54:56,580 --> 00:54:57,202
Rym: Hmm.

894
00:54:57,242 --> 00:54:57,984
Rym: Could be a hot dog.

895
00:54:59,247 --> 00:55:02,876
Scott: You know, what if I make little rice balls instead of a big flat rice noodle?

896
00:55:03,076 --> 00:55:04,500
Rym: I'd say that's a dumpling then.

897
00:55:05,281 --> 00:55:05,602
Scott: It could be.

898
00:55:05,622 --> 00:55:08,067
Rym: Of course, in that case, you're going to want to cook it.

899
00:55:08,087 --> 00:55:14,220
Scott: Meanwhile, an actual dumpling, when you think about a dumpling, right, is like it's a flat rice noodle and then you wrap something with it.

900
00:55:14,440 --> 00:55:17,888
Scott: And now the shape is because you sealed something inside.

901
00:55:17,949 --> 00:55:20,935
Scott: But actually the wrapper part is flat.

902
00:55:21,056 --> 00:55:22,880
Rym: And that's kind of depends on what kind of dumpling.

903
00:55:22,960 --> 00:55:23,181
Rym: What about?

904
00:55:23,201 --> 00:55:30,460
Rym: there's the kind of dumpling where you basically drip a blob of some sort of British substance into, say, a soup.

905
00:55:31,720 --> 00:55:35,950
Rym: And then it hardens and solidifies and cooks and becomes a dumpling.

906
00:55:35,991 --> 00:55:37,153
Rym: It's a very different kind of dumpling.

907
00:55:37,173 --> 00:55:38,076
Rym: I don't.

908
00:55:38,156 --> 00:55:39,880
Rym: actually I haven't made those in a while and I should.

909
00:55:40,640 --> 00:55:42,547
Scott: Is that how you make a gnocchi from scratch?

910
00:55:42,587 --> 00:55:45,538
Scott: You take like potato dough and drip it into water?

911
00:55:46,400 --> 00:55:47,284
Rym: I don't know.

912
00:55:47,405 --> 00:55:50,900
Rym: I've only ever bought gnocchis that were pre-made and cooked them.

913
00:55:51,420 --> 00:55:55,939
Scott: I have never thought to look up how to make a gnocchi because I too have only ever bought pre-made gnocchi.

914
00:55:56,241 --> 00:55:58,729
Rym: I have never made fresh pasta since I was a kid.

915
00:55:58,749 --> 00:56:01,198
Rym: I don't even have the stuff to make it.

916
00:56:02,260 --> 00:56:02,361
Scott: Yeah.

917
00:56:02,381 --> 00:56:04,412
Scott: The thing about making pasta is pretty easy.

918
00:56:04,432 --> 00:56:05,458
Scott: You just make a dough, right?

919
00:56:06,361 --> 00:56:06,463
Scott: Yeah.

920
00:56:06,483 --> 00:56:07,380
Rym: Extrude it for something.

921
00:56:08,220 --> 00:56:08,300
Scott: Yeah.

922
00:56:08,320 --> 00:56:09,443
Scott: And then you have to shaping.

923
00:56:09,484 --> 00:56:10,466
Scott: it is where the work is.

924
00:56:10,547 --> 00:56:10,767
Scott: Right.

925
00:56:10,787 --> 00:56:15,240
Scott: And on the one hand, it's like you could just go with a simple shape with a pasta machine.

926
00:56:16,221 --> 00:56:18,207
Scott: But it's actually kind of more work than it.

927
00:56:18,267 --> 00:56:20,454
Scott: Like whenever you watch videos, you're like, that looks easy.

928
00:56:20,815 --> 00:56:22,280
Scott: And then you try to do it yourself.

929
00:56:22,400 --> 00:56:27,420
Scott: And it's like, oh, I have to crank this thing a lot and keep running the dough through it a lot.

930
00:56:27,801 --> 00:56:31,240
Scott: And then it's like it turns into a whole arts and crafts project, the shaping part.

931
00:56:31,420 --> 00:56:42,700
Scott: It's like you see those people who are like, you know, noodle experts who will like cut the noodle in half, fold it, cut it in half, fold it, cut it, fold it, cut it, fold it to make like a million noodles out of one dough blob.

932
00:56:43,321 --> 00:56:45,126
Scott: And it's like they make it look so easy.

933
00:56:45,146 --> 00:56:45,988
Scott: You go try to do that.

934
00:56:46,008 --> 00:56:48,033
Scott: It's a pain in the ass, right?

935
00:56:48,274 --> 00:56:50,480
Scott: To hold it correctly so that it doesn't become a big mess.

936
00:56:50,640 --> 00:56:53,145
Scott: And also it's heavy and you got to lift the whole thing up and fold.

937
00:56:53,185 --> 00:56:53,426
Scott: Right.

938
00:56:53,787 --> 00:56:58,215
Scott: It's like, you know, it's one of those things where they make it look easy, but it ain't easy.

939
00:56:59,999 --> 00:57:00,420
Scott: It's not.

